INT. FLOODED COMMAND DUGOUT, WESTERN FRONT - DAWN, 1917

Timber props. Water to the ankles. A field telephone dangles from a nail. Shells rumble, miles off.

MAJOR ALISTAIR PEMBROKE-HUNT (40s, immaculate despite the mud, pipe unlit) sits at a map table. Across from him, LIEUTENANT SAMUEL ODUYA (20s, shaking hands, cracked spectacles) stands at attention. In the corner, SERGEANT DESMOND ARCHIBALD (50s, scarred jaw, rifle across his lap) polishes a bayonet.

PEMBROKE-HUNT
Sit, Lieutenant.

ODUYA
I'll stand, sir.

PEMBROKE-HUNT
Sit.

Oduya sits. The water around his boots ripples.

PEMBROKE-HUNT (CONT'D)
At six o'clock, you'll take your platoon over the top.

ODUYA
Sir, the wire isn't cut.

PEMBROKE-HUNT
It will be.

ODUYA
Sir, with respect, the artillery has been wrong three days running.

PEMBROKE-HUNT
Lieutenant, I did not ask for a critique.

ODUYA
No, sir. But my men will ask me why. And I won't have an answer.

ARCHIBALD
(quietly, not looking up)
Tell them it's an order, lad. It's the only answer anyone's got.

PEMBROKE-HUNT
Thank you, Sergeant.

ARCHIBALD
Don't thank me, sir. I'm only telling the boy what I told the last one.

Pembroke-Hunt sets down his pencil. For a long moment, the only sound is dripping.

PEMBROKE-HUNT
Lieutenant Oduya. Do you have family?

ODUYA
A mother, sir. In Lagos. And a fiancee in Kent.

PEMBROKE-HUNT
Write to them now. I'll see the letters go out whatever happens.

ODUYA
Thank you, sir.

PEMBROKE-HUNT
That's all.

Oduya rises, salutes, and wades toward the ladder. Archibald watches him go, then turns to the Major.

ARCHIBALD
You'll be writing his, sir?

PEMBROKE-HUNT
(after a pause)
I always do.
